  HONEST READ (exactly as it must be stated): LCDM halo fits FIT WELL -- with 2-3
  free parameters per galaxy they reach a lower total chi2, and on raw AIC/BIC per
  -point fit quality they can win despite the parameter penalty. The economy contrast
  is FREEDOM COUNT and PREDICTIVITY: the framework's number was fixed by the CMB
  before any rotation curve was looked at (1 global convention parameter, ZERO per-
  object parameters, and the SAME number must simultaneously survive P2/P3/N1-N4);
  the halo alternative spends hundreds of per-object parameters and carries NO
  cross-probe number to thread. AIC/BIC computed on one dataset cannot price that
  cross-dataset rigidity -- that is what the crossing figure shows.
  CHI2 CAVEAT (both directions): the framework row models NO per-galaxy nuisance at
  all -- SPARC's own 10-30% distance and inclination uncertainties enter its chi2 as
  unmodeled error, while per-galaxy halo parameters partially absorb them. The
  like-for-like single-statistic comparison stays the committed RAR row: 0.108 dex
  (framework, canonical a0, 1 global Upsilon) vs 0.122 (reg-MOND) on the same points.

